course: adult-life
lesson: 18
title: We're having a baby!
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When a woman is pregnant, a baby grows inside her womb for about nine months before it is born. Grown-ups often say "we're expecting" instead of "pregnant".
explain: Many parents wait about three months before telling people, because the early weeks are the most uncertain.
explain: In Melbourne, Mum smiles at Dad over dinner and says, "We've got some news. We're expecting." Soon there is a grey fuzzy scan picture on the fridge, a date circled on the calendar and a list of names nobody can agree on.
explain: Midwives and doctors check on mum and baby all the way through. Usually all goes well, but sometimes a pregnancy ends early and the baby is lost, which is very sad and nobody's fault.
fact: "We're expecting" means a baby is growing inside Mum and will be born in a few months.
fact: "The bump" is the round belly of a pregnant woman, made by the baby, the water around it and the stretched womb.
fact: You should always ask before touching someone's bump.
fact: A scan picture is made by a machine that uses sound waves to see the baby inside the body without hurting.
fact: Morning sickness is feeling or being sick in early pregnancy, and despite the name it can happen at any time of day.
fact: Morning sickness usually gets better after about three or four months, and it is not catching.
fact: The due date is the doctors' best guess of the birth day, and babies can come up to two weeks before or after it.
fact: "It's time, we're going to the hospital" means labour has started and the baby is getting ready to be born.
fact: Labour can take many hours, so a grandparent or friend often comes to look after the older children.
fact: A midwife is a nurse trained to care for women and babies during pregnancy, birth and the first days after.
fact: Parents often make a shortlist of names, and the name must be registered officially within a few weeks of the birth.
fact: A miscarriage is when a baby stops growing and dies early in pregnancy; it happens quite often and is nobody's fault.
fact: Twins are two babies from one pregnancy; identical twins come from one egg that split, so they look alike.
exercise: Mum says, "We've got some news. We're expecting." What does it mean? | A baby is growing inside her | She is expecting a parcel | She is waiting for a phone call | Visitors are coming to stay
exercise: Why do many parents wait about three months before telling people about a baby? | The early weeks are the most uncertain | It is against the law to tell anyone any sooner | The baby cannot be seen before then | They want to keep it a surprise forever
exercise: Everyone calls Auntie's round tummy "the bump". What is it? | Her belly, grown round by the baby inside | A bruise from bumping into the kitchen table | A swelling from eating too much food at Christmas | A cushion she wears for a costume
exercise: You want to feel the baby kick in Auntie's bump. What should you do first? | Ask her if it is okay | Just put your hand on it | Press hard to wake the baby | Shout at the bump so it moves
exercise: What is the grey fuzzy picture on the fridge? | A scan of the baby made with sound waves | An X-ray of Mum's bones taken at the dentist's | A photo taken through Mum's skin | A drawing the doctor made by hand
exercise: Mum is sick in the afternoon and says it is morning sickness. How can that be? | Morning sickness can happen at any time of day | She must be mistaken about the time | It only counts if it starts first thing in the morning | It means she has caught a tummy bug
exercise: Mum has morning sickness. What is true? | It is not catching and it usually passes | You will catch it if you hug her or sit close to her | It lasts for the whole nine months | It means the baby is ill
exercise: Dad says, "The due date is the fourteenth of March." What is it? | The doctors' best guess of the birth day | The exact day the baby must be born, by law | The day Mum goes back to work | The day the baby gets its name
exercise: The baby arrives ten days after the due date. Is something wrong? | No, babies can come up to two weeks either side | Yes, babies must come on the due date | Yes, it means the doctors made a big mistake with their sums | No, babies always come a month late
exercise: Dad grabs a packed bag at night: "It's time, we're going to the hospital." What is happening? | Labour has started and the baby is coming | Dad has hurt himself and needs a doctor | They are going for a scan picture | They are visiting a friend in hospital
exercise: You wake up and Gran is in the kitchen instead of your parents. Why, most likely? | Mum is in labour and Gran is minding you | Your parents have gone on holiday without you | Gran has moved in for good | Your parents forgot to tell you about a party
exercise: "The midwife is coming round." Who is she? | A nurse trained to care for mums and babies | A cleaner who helps with the housework after the birth | A teacher for the older children | A friend from Mum's work
exercise: "We've got a shortlist of names." What does it mean? | They have a few names and have not chosen yet | They want a very short name for the baby, like Al or Jo | They have already registered the name | They want you to choose the name alone
exercise: Mum says quietly, "There isn't going to be a baby after all. We lost it." What happened? | A miscarriage: the baby died early in pregnancy | The baby was born and then given away | The hospital has lost all of Mum's notes and paperwork | The baby will just come a lot later
exercise: Was the miscarriage anyone's fault? | No, it happens often and usually nobody knows why | Yes, Mum must have done something wrong, like lifting too much | Yes, it was because you were noisy | Yes, the midwife was not careful enough
exercise: The midwife says, "There are two of them. It's twins!" What does it mean? | Two babies are growing in one pregnancy | The scan machine is broken and showing it twice | Mum is having a baby now and one next year | One baby and its shadow on the picture
exercise: What are identical twins? | Twins from one egg that split, so they look alike | Any twins who wear the same clothes | Twins born on the same day of the week | Twins who have the same first name
test: Overheard on the phone: "Twelve weeks today, so we can finally tell people!" What is the news? | A pregnancy has passed the early weeks | Someone's new job starts on Monday | A holiday is booked in twelve weeks | Someone has been ill for twelve weeks
test: Your brother says, "Morning sickness means Mum ate something bad at breakfast." Put him right. | It is sickness from early pregnancy, at any time | He is right, it is food poisoning from the eggs | He is right, she should skip breakfast | It means she caught it from you
test: A girl in Glasgow says, "The baby's due Friday, so it'll definitely be born Friday." Which is true? | It may come up to two weeks either side | Yes, babies are always born on the due date | Yes, if the doctors say so it must happen on that day | No, babies always come a month early
test: Gran texts at 3am: "Here now, go back to sleep pet, Mum and Dad at hospital." What is going on? | Mum is in labour and Gran is minding you | Gran has had an accident at home | Mum and Dad are at a work party | The house has had a burglary
test: Aunt in Sydney: "Don't you dare poke my bump, ask first!" Why? | It is her body, and you should ask before touching | The bump will pop like a balloon if you poke it | The baby will be born straight away | Poking makes the baby turn into twins
test: Mum and Dad bickering: "We are NOT calling her Beyoncé." "It's on the shortlist!" What is going on? | They are choosing a name from a list | They are arguing about a singer's latest song | They have already named the baby | They are planning a birthday party
test: Your sister whispers, "Mum lost the baby because I jumped on her bed." Put her right. | It is nobody's fault, and certainly not hers | She is right, she should feel guilty | She is right, jumping causes it every time | It was the midwife's fault, not hers
test: Dad, pointing at the fridge: "See that blob? That's the head." What is he showing? | A scan picture of the baby | A photo of the baby already born | An X-ray of Dad's broken arm | A weather map from the news
test: News report: "The number of twin births has risen in recent years." What are twin births? | Two babies born from one pregnancy | Babies born on the same day in a town | Babies who share a birthday with a parent | Mothers who have had two babies in two years
test: Mum, after a midwife visit: "She weighed him and checked I'm healing, all good." What did the midwife do? | Checked on the new baby and on Mum | Cleaned the house from top to bottom for the family | Taught the older children maths | Brought presents for the new baby
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression "We are expecting"? | It indicates that a pregnancy is in progress | It indicates that a delivery of goods is anticipated | It indicates that a visitor is due to arrive | It indicates that a decision is awaited
exam: Identify the principal function of the scan conducted during pregnancy. | To check by means of sound waves that the baby is developing normally | To administer medicine to the unborn baby | To determine the baby's future height precisely | To photograph the baby by means of ordinary light shone through the skin
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the due date. | It is an estimate, and delivery may occur up to two weeks before or after it | It is the precise date upon which delivery must occur | It is the date upon which the baby's name is registered | It is the date upon which the mother returns to her employment after the birth
exam: Whereas a pregnancy has ended in miscarriage, which of the following statements is accurate? | Such an occurrence is relatively common and ordinarily attributable to no one | Such an occurrence is invariably caused by the mother's conduct during the pregnancy | Such an occurrence is caused by the conduct of other children | Such an occurrence signifies that the baby will be born later
